   "text": {
    "L1": "Preserving food means keeping it from spoiling for longer.",
    "L2": "Microbes can spoil food, so preservation stops or slows their growth.",
    "L3": "Drying removes water. Salt and sugar pull water out, so microbes cannot grow easily.",
    "L4": "Oil keeps air and moisture away. Boiling kills many microbes, and airtight packs keep new microbes out.",
    "L5": "A fridge only slows microbes; it does not kill them. Freezing slows them much more.",
    "L6": "So fridge food can still spoil after some days. Check its smell, look and expiry date, and never taste it to test it.",
    "L7": "For example, papad is dried, achaar uses salt and oil, and milk can be boiled and kept cold.",
    "L8": "Now choose the method that removes the water microbes need."
   },
